When you think strength training and exercising kids, the kid centric approach would be movement literacy.

00:16:13.752 --> 00:16:20.136
The primary goal of strength training for kids should be to enhance movement literacy.

00:16:20.328 --> 00:16:24.664
Now, that is a very specific phrase and we use it that way intentionally.

00:16:24.792 --> 00:16:27.448
I just want you to be more literate with movement.

00:16:27.544 --> 00:16:34.858
What that means better locomotion, more coordination, more control, better stability.

00:16:35.034 --> 00:16:38.234
Move how you want and don't move how you don't want.

00:16:38.402 --> 00:16:46.990
Movement options, movement variety, movement choice, unique and independent and creative ways to solve movement problems. That's movement literacy.

00:16:47.570 --> 00:16:49.550
That is strength training for kids.

00:16:50.210 --> 00:16:52.442
It's not an hour and a half calf workout.

00:16:52.506 --> 00:16:56.250
It's not maximizing strength or powerlifting or competing.

00:16:56.410 --> 00:17:02.002
So hopefully that alone cleared up one of the biggest misconceptions we've got in this entire

00:17:02.066 --> 00:17:06.610
field, that when we say strength, that we're meaning things like that when we're actually meaning

00:17:06.770 --> 00:17:15.030
almost always movement literacy in kids, movement, quality, motor control, coordination.

00:17:15.930 --> 00:17:19.858
They're not direct synonyms for strength, but they're pretty darn close.

00:17:19.914 --> 00:17:21.794
