01:04:48.706 --> 01:04:53.674
I think he calls them speed demons or something at this age. But it's still fundamentals.

01:04:53.722 --> 01:04:58.186
We're expanding past the baby or the toddler age and we're doing the same kind of stuff.

01:04:58.218 --> 01:05:00.426
But now we're making competitive games.

01:05:00.578 --> 01:05:05.770
So we're doing some relay races, we're competing against a sibling or a peer.

01:05:05.850 --> 01:05:11.226
Sometimes we're jumping over hurdles, we're doing multiple step scenarios.

01:05:11.418 --> 01:05:15.012
So this is jump over a hurdle and then spin around in a circle.

01:05:15.106 --> 01:05:18.832
It is an army crawl under three benches and then into a hula hoop.

01:05:18.896 --> 01:05:22.608
It is get on all hands and knees while you're holding that position.

01:05:22.744 --> 01:05:26.304
Or you're maybe doing a bear crawl while also dodge a ball.

01:05:26.392 --> 01:05:27.408
How do you dodge the ball?

01:05:27.464 --> 01:05:31.008
You could duck it, you could roll, you could flip over, you could do a somersault.

01:05:31.104 --> 01:05:35.504
You can see it's multiple steps combined and it's not, it's not a drill, right.

01:05:35.592 --> 01:05:36.848
It's just a different activity.

01:05:36.944 --> 01:05:38.592
It's jump up and touch an object.

01:05:38.656 --> 01:05:45.100
It is attention, it is specific, it is multiple steps and so forth. Right.

01:05:45.140 --> 01:05:49.324
So that would be general fundamental principles at this point.

01:05:49.412 --> 01:05:53.308
We would now cross into again here in America what we call the kind of middle school.

01:05:53.444 --> 01:05:57.900
This is 11 years old to 14 year olds. Just broad ranges here. Right.

01:05:58.020 --> 01:06:02.000
And now we're actually learning to be a multifaceted mover.

01:06:02.340 --> 01:06:06.290
So we're able to do probably more traditional strength conditioning at this point.

01:06:06.460 --> 01:06:11.030
Now just to be really clear, not many people are going to advocate waiting until you're 11 or

01:06:11.070 --> 01:06:13.350
12 to start strength training.

01:06:13.510 --> 01:06:17.958
The general accepted position with strength training, as I said earlier, but to be really clear,

01:06:18.094 --> 01:06:24.662
you can start lifting weights as soon as the child is mature enough to pay attention to, want

01:06:24.686 --> 01:06:26.970
to do it and to follow instructions.

01:06:27.310 --> 01:06:33.200
Another way to think about this, if you can play an organized sport, you're probably ready to do some strength training.

01:06:33.390 --> 01:06:34.920
Now what does that look like?

01:06:35.220 --> 01:06:37.964
Exactly what I'm talking about earlier, your strength training.

01:06:38.012 --> 01:06:42.508
Things like, you know, jumping, pounding, rolling, that's still strength training.

01:06:42.604 --> 01:06:43.964
So they're still doing those things.

01:06:44.052 --> 01:06:48.988
If you want to use kettlebells and dumbbells and barbells and machines, great, you can do that too.

01:06:49.124 --> 01:06:53.996
But it's the same kind of movements here, ideally as most of our training.

01:06:54.108 --> 01:06:57.724
Okay, so we only want to point that out because I didn't want people to think we're advocating

01:06:57.772 --> 01:07:02.874
for only starting traditional weight training at 11 years old or 12 years old.

01:07:02.962 --> 01:07:07.642
You can start as soon as the person, the kid wants to, as soon as they can pay attention to safety rules.

01:07:07.706 --> 01:07:11.722
You're not going to obviously go after maximum strength or muscle growth and things like that,

01:07:11.826 --> 01:07:13.322
but you get the basic point.

01:07:13.426 --> 01:07:19.190
Okay, so moving on with again Jeremy's and colleagues, rough model here.

01:07:19.570 --> 01:07:25.434
Once we are in this 11 to 14 year old range, it looks probably more like a traditional strength and conditioning environment.

01:07:25.602 --> 01:07:32.970
We are more organized, there's less freedom in many aspects of the training, but there's still a ton of variety. Right.

01:07:33.010 --> 01:07:35.034
You're not just doing the same couple of exercises.

01:07:35.162 --> 01:07:37.670
Your coaching technique very hard at this point.

01:07:38.770 --> 01:07:43.402
You start a coaching technique a little bit earlier, but you're really, you know, specific and

01:07:43.426 --> 01:07:48.858
focused on it now and you're doing more direct sporting related activities and exercises.

01:07:48.954 --> 01:07:54.950
More hopping, more skipping, more jumping, more lunges, more squats and hinges and things like that.

01:07:55.410 --> 01:08:01.034
Once you get into early high school, this overlaps again like with those previous ltad models.

01:08:01.162 --> 01:08:03.818
Early high school is when you're now becoming an athlete.

01:08:03.914 --> 01:08:08.330
So we are competing, we're trying to get stronger, we're trying to get faster, we are working

01:08:08.370 --> 01:08:11.258
on skill development of the individual sports and activities.

01:08:11.434 --> 01:08:17.344
Once you get to kind of late high school, this would be sort of 16, 17, 18 years old range.

01:08:17.522 --> 01:08:20.196
You can become optimized for that sport.

01:08:20.308 --> 01:08:26.484
We can start really competing, emphasizing winning, what it takes to be strategic tactics, techniques,

01:08:26.612 --> 01:08:32.280
game plans, specialization, focusing on one sport now and so on and so forth.

01:08:32.620 --> 01:08:40.068
If you make it to college or sometimes you're any situation past high school now we're we're elite into specialization.

01:08:40.164 --> 01:08:45.399
You're basically optimizing everything you're doing for that individual sport or activity that you've chosen to.

01:08:45.699 --> 01:08:51.839
The final step, like always for every ltad model is then life and getting back to the basics.

01:08:52.339 --> 01:08:59.723
Lacking specialization, opening movement back up, being less precise with the programming and

01:08:59.891 --> 01:09:02.507
more varied, more movement patterns and so forth.

01:09:02.523 --> 01:09:07.787
So you can actually kind of see you're going back to the basics in one way to think about it.

01:09:07.923 --> 01:09:14.286
If you want to think about some more specifics in terms of the exercise selection, we generally

01:09:14.318 --> 01:09:17.890
would call this movement skills rather than exercises.

01:09:18.390 --> 01:09:21.166
Again, child centric versus adult centric.

01:09:21.358 --> 01:09:23.326
Just a handful of concepts to think about.

01:09:23.398 --> 01:09:28.862
First one is, which I said one moment ago, you want to think about games rather than drills.

01:09:29.006 --> 01:09:34.062
An adult, you're going to go do this exercise a kid, we're going to Play this game, okay, in

01:09:34.086 --> 01:09:38.175
both how you're speaking to the person as well as how you're actually treating it, you're building

01:09:38.247 --> 01:09:40.419
games versus doing an exercise.

01:09:41.079 --> 01:09:44.415
Number two, you want to let them solve those movement problems themselves.

01:09:44.527 --> 01:09:46.623
They want to figure out what's faster, what's slower.

01:09:46.751 --> 01:09:50.431
Where an adult you're going to be coaching, you're going to go right to the techniques that

01:09:50.455 --> 01:09:53.099
you know are going to be work, most likely for their body.

01:09:53.879 --> 01:10:00.623
Number three, in terms of the loading, you want to use a variety, especially doing your warmups.

01:10:00.671 --> 01:10:03.967
And so you really want to hit them with tons of different movements.

01:10:04.063 --> 01:10:10.880
Way more than you want to use a lot of variety with adults, but way more in children than you do later in life.

01:10:11.820 --> 01:10:14.800
Range of motion should be exceptionally high.

01:10:15.500 --> 01:10:20.228
One thing we want to be really careful of is not many coaches are going to spend a lot of time

01:10:20.284 --> 01:10:22.692
specifically working on flexibility in kids.

01:10:22.836 --> 01:10:24.800
And you don't necessarily need to.

01:10:25.100 --> 01:10:29.988
You want to build range of motion through the activities that you're going after, rather than

01:10:30.044 --> 01:10:31.816
sitting down and doing static stretching.

01:10:31.988 --> 01:10:35.728
If you're doing a lot of variety and a lot of movement pains in the way we were discussing earlier,

01:10:35.784 --> 01:10:37.792
the range of motion should take care of itself.

01:10:37.936 --> 01:10:42.000
Obviously, the younger the kid, the less organized you are with range of motion.

01:10:42.080 --> 01:10:46.832
The wider range of activities, the older they get, the more organized you need to get, the more

01:10:46.856 --> 01:10:48.208
specific you need to get.

01:10:48.344 --> 01:10:53.216
You need to be doing dynamic warmups, but maybe now you're incorporating some static stretching

01:10:53.248 --> 01:10:58.298
based on an individual's needs or limitations or movement pattern restrictions or so forth.

01:10:58.394 --> 01:11:03.754
But the default setting for kids is a large range of motion possible.

01:11:03.922 --> 01:11:08.618
Once you get into those later high school years and you're becoming more specialized.

01:11:08.794 --> 01:11:13.994
If you want to change and alter or shorten a range of motion for a very specific goal or outcome, that's fine.

01:11:14.042 --> 01:11:18.730
But at this stage, we're not optimizing for sport, so we should be optimizing for range of motion

01:11:18.890 --> 01:11:21.630
most of the time as our default strategy and setting.

01:11:22.130 --> 01:11:27.070
Last thing to think about conceptually, for these basic movement pattern skill development tools,

01:11:28.130 --> 01:11:30.858
you're rarely doing exercises at high volume.

01:11:31.034 --> 01:11:36.442
You want to go low volume, high intent, very moderate to low fatigue.

01:11:36.506 --> 01:11:39.530
Most of the time, you can do some fatiguing stuff. If it's.

01:11:39.610 --> 01:11:43.402
If, say, you're playing a game of dodgeball and kids are, you know, working really hard for

01:11:43.426 --> 01:11:45.370
10 minutes, that's your conditioning, right?

01:11:45.410 --> 01:11:48.842
Or you're doing some other game there where there's some fatigue associated.

01:11:48.986 --> 01:11:54.306
But most of the exercise, most of the lifting, most of the drills, there are a couple of seconds,

01:11:54.418 --> 01:11:56.018
there are a few repetitions.

01:11:56.194 --> 01:12:01.922
You're trying really hard, you're going really fast, you're focusing, trying to win, and you're resting and recovering.

01:12:02.066 --> 01:12:06.870
The goal is not at this point in their life to develop fatigue resistance.

01:12:07.290 --> 01:12:11.010
We're not trying to enhance fatigue resistance at this stage of life.

01:12:11.050 --> 01:12:12.930
We're trying to develop better skills.

01:12:13.090 --> 01:12:18.292
That means they need to be somewhat fresh, they need a lot of practice, a lot of repetitions at it.

01:12:18.386 --> 01:12:24.352
They need a lot of repetitions at a lot of different scenarios, setups, situations and implements.

01:12:24.496 --> 01:12:26.528
And they need to do it with high intent, right?

01:12:26.584 --> 01:12:27.900
Figure out what works for them.

01:12:28.200 --> 01:12:34.512
So those are the big concepts, some of the targets in terms of the skill development, things

01:12:34.616 --> 01:12:37.020
you want to go after a balance. We said that earlier.

01:12:37.560 --> 01:12:40.848
Something people don't talk about a lot though is grappling.

01:12:40.944 --> 01:12:43.892
Grappling is a very important skill for kids.

01:12:44.016 --> 01:12:48.412
This is pushing, this is pulling, this is lifting and carrying.

01:12:48.556 --> 01:12:50.988
We would collectively again call that grappling.

01:12:51.164 --> 01:12:59.580
Locomotion is everything from sprinting and running and agility, jumping and landing, two legged, one legged, rotating.

01:12:59.660 --> 01:13:03.996
So jump and turn and land, jumping on things, jumping off of things.

01:13:04.068 --> 01:13:06.640
These are actually fundamental skills for kids.

01:13:07.380 --> 01:13:13.110
Tumbling, rolling, side rolls, shoulder rolls, butt rolls, all of those things are important.

01:13:13.410 --> 01:13:16.586
You also want to incorporate target practice.

01:13:16.698 --> 01:13:21.322
So be able to throw, be able to catch, be able to hit something, be able to aim at something

01:13:21.426 --> 01:13:23.658
with the upper body and the lower body.

01:13:23.794 --> 01:13:25.642
Kids often get this with the upper body, right?

01:13:25.666 --> 01:13:29.338
They're going to throw a football at something or try to hit something, but make sure they're

01:13:29.354 --> 01:13:32.310
getting those same skills with the lower body as well.

01:13:32.850 --> 01:13:38.532
Climbing, very specifically, whether this is hanging from a bar, this is rock climbing, this

01:13:38.556 --> 01:13:41.880
is ninja warrior or parkour or climbing fences.

01:13:42.460 --> 01:13:49.252
Fundamental movement skills that are a little bit different than everything that we've talked about thus far. Pay attention to rhythm.

01:13:49.396 --> 01:13:54.996
I said this really briefly earlier, but this is something that is oftentimes forgotten. So this is often.

01:13:55.068 --> 01:13:58.260
You think about this in drills where you have to do multiple things at different speeds.

01:13:58.340 --> 01:14:03.004
So imagine like a three part drill or game, I should say, Sorry, violated my own rule there,

01:14:03.132 --> 01:14:07.852
where the first part, they have to step over some hurdles that are pretty high.

01:14:08.036 --> 01:14:11.212
Now they can't do that very fast, so they're going at one particular speed.

01:14:11.276 --> 01:14:16.812
They finish that and then they sprint 15 yards backpedal, 15 yards back. That'll be really fast.

01:14:16.956 --> 01:14:21.480
And then they get that and they do a fast foot drill on a ladder on the ground.

01:14:21.780 --> 01:14:23.644
Those are all gonna Happen at three different speeds.

01:14:23.692 --> 01:14:28.332
So they're practicing, learning to try as hard as they can to try to go fast.

01:14:28.396 --> 01:14:33.790
But the actual rate of movement, the rhythm, big steps, small steps, change of pace, not change

01:14:33.830 --> 01:14:36.750
of pace, open up the throttle, hold things back.

01:14:36.870 --> 01:14:46.478
These are all how you develop rhythm of movement, which is critical to overall skill development. Spatial awareness, judging distance. Right.

01:14:46.534 --> 01:14:48.142
So being able to throw an implement.

01:14:48.206 --> 01:14:53.374
I actually was, funny enough doing this with my kids very recently, where you take an object

01:14:53.422 --> 01:14:56.302
and put them at many different distances and they have to learn to throw.

01:14:56.446 --> 01:14:59.662
In our case, we were throwing a medicine ball at, we were knocking something over.

01:14:59.766 --> 01:15:03.102
But one of them was short, one of them was a long ways away, one of them was close.

01:15:03.126 --> 01:15:09.070
And so they're starting to realize and figure out how do I manage force input to give me a very specific distance.

01:15:09.230 --> 01:15:12.942
Same thing with their own body, figuring out how close I am to another kid.

01:15:13.006 --> 01:15:16.690
So we'll do activities where we're in close environments and you can't hit somebody.

01:15:17.190 --> 01:15:24.884
So spatial awareness, kinesthetic awareness, distance judging, hitting a ball that's coming at you. Right. Hitting a volleyball.

01:15:25.012 --> 01:15:26.452
You get the idea, right.

01:15:26.636 --> 01:15:28.932
Reaction time is another independent one.

01:15:29.036 --> 01:15:30.880
You all know what reaction time is.

01:15:31.180 --> 01:15:36.260
And then a similar one, but that's actually uniquely different, is what we actually call decision making with speed.

01:15:36.420 --> 01:15:41.044
And so this is something like, you know, you're going to, your coach is going to give you signal

01:15:41.092 --> 01:15:44.468
and you have to interpret that signal and make a decision fast as you possibly can.

01:15:44.524 --> 01:15:47.044
This happens naturally in many sports, but not all.

01:15:47.132 --> 01:15:51.382
But that's a different thing than just reaction alone because now we're enhancing the cognitive demand.

01:15:51.556 --> 01:15:55.630
Make the right choice and then give me the physical reaction as well.

01:15:56.090 --> 01:16:01.710
I could go on and on, but that was 11 or 12 or 13 different targets to go after.

01:16:02.250 --> 01:16:07.458
So as you're either designing your own program or you're really watching your kids program,

01:16:07.594 --> 01:16:08.946
you're watching what your kids are doing.

01:16:09.018 --> 01:16:14.466
You can use a list like this and go, hey, geez, we're only doing, we haven't done any tumbling in forever.

01:16:14.578 --> 01:16:17.514
We don't do any climbing, we haven't done any spatial awareness.

01:16:17.602 --> 01:16:22.330
And then you maybe you can add something, one or two things to what your kids are doing overall

01:16:22.410 --> 01:16:26.474
and give them a more well rounded physical training approach.

01:16:26.642 --> 01:16:29.630
That's the best way, I think, to use information like I just covered.

01:16:30.370 --> 01:16:33.370
Look at what your kid is doing and what you're having them do or what your coaches are doing.

01:16:33.410 --> 01:16:37.514
Then it may be, say, ah, geez, we have some big things we haven't filled in there and see if

01:16:37.522 --> 01:16:42.750
we can add a drill here and there or a game here and there or an activity at home to fill in some gaps.

01:16:43.100 --> 01:16:47.908
One of the things that's also nice about a list like this, as I said at the beginning, none

01:16:47.924 --> 01:16:49.480
of this takes special equipment.

01:16:49.900 --> 01:16:55.332
None of this takes highly trained skilled coaches or trainers. Everyone can do this.

01:16:55.436 --> 01:16:57.360
Not every kid will be great at all these things.

01:16:57.660 --> 01:17:00.080
You shouldn't do this all every day.

01:17:00.620 --> 01:17:05.780
But almost anyone in the world, regardless of access, can look at a list like that and just

01:17:05.820 --> 01:17:07.268
try to get a bunch of these things done.

01:17:07.324 --> 01:17:12.106
And if you were to do that and you were to do it often, as often as you can or as you're willing

01:17:12.138 --> 01:17:16.170
to do, it's going to be a huge help and you're going to get most of the things we talked about

01:17:16.210 --> 01:17:24.550
earlier for overall physical development and be in a really good spot with free or zero cost for you at all.

01:17:24.850 --> 01:17:28.810
I think it'd be nice for me to cover one actual direct example.

01:17:28.970 --> 01:17:34.602
The way I'm going to do that is I looked at an actual program from Jeremy that he was able to

01:17:34.626 --> 01:17:41.096
share and I said, okay, if I were to take the concepts and apply it to one individual kid, what could that look like?

01:17:41.168 --> 01:17:47.704
So I've sketched up a program here based on again, Jeremy's actual program as well as some concepts, made some modifications.

01:17:47.752 --> 01:17:53.656
And I just said, if I had a 7 year old, what would one day of a workout look like for that 7 year old?

01:17:53.808 --> 01:18:00.312
I like Jeremy's approach because in that model he has everything from what is the drill, what

01:18:00.336 --> 01:18:05.918
is the intent, what's the coaching cues and strategy, how long is it going to take and what's

01:18:05.934 --> 01:18:08.846
the actual outcome is really high, really highly organized stuff.

01:18:08.878 --> 01:18:12.638
I'm not going to share all that with you, just drag on a little bit.

01:18:12.774 --> 01:18:14.078
But I love that part of the model.

01:18:14.094 --> 01:18:15.326
So I'll share with you the first couple.

01:18:15.438 --> 01:18:18.846
So let's say the kid walks in. 7 year old kid, right?

01:18:18.998 --> 01:18:23.214
Nothing else we know about the individual, but seven years old, roughly, we're always going

01:18:23.222 --> 01:18:24.814
to start with some sort of warm up.

01:18:24.982 --> 01:18:31.528
Now the goal to warm up, balance, stability and reaction time in addition to having some fun.

01:18:31.664 --> 01:18:35.128
Again, you could switch out these goals, but what is the goal? Okay.

01:18:35.144 --> 01:18:37.080
Therefore we know those are the four goals we identified.

01:18:37.160 --> 01:18:41.740
How long we can invest. Ten minutes. Got it. What's the coaching cue?

01:18:42.080 --> 01:18:45.020
You want to be relaxed and semi structural.

01:18:45.680 --> 01:18:51.352
So you one more time as the parent or the coach or the individual going through it, they're warming Up.

01:18:51.456 --> 01:18:53.880
You want to coach, but you don't want to be all on them.

01:18:53.920 --> 01:18:55.656
You know, really grilling them with the details.

01:18:55.688 --> 01:19:01.034
So it's relaxed, get things going and semi structured, but a lot of freedom as well.

01:19:01.202 --> 01:19:07.594
What are the actual exercises ones that I saw actually videos of Jeremy doing one called a get up to hop.

01:19:07.682 --> 01:19:12.714
This is the kid is laying on the belly and either the kid does it or on a, you know, a stopwatch

01:19:12.762 --> 01:19:14.730
or a start or you know, ready, go.

01:19:14.850 --> 01:19:18.634
The kid gets up from their belly and goes immediately to one leg and starts hopping on one leg.

01:19:18.802 --> 01:19:20.426
So it's a multiple step approach.

01:19:20.578 --> 01:19:25.314
He showed examples of doing that as well with a hop to a turn or hop to a twist, hop to one

01:19:25.322 --> 01:19:26.290
leg, hop to another one.

01:19:26.330 --> 01:19:29.426
But it's a multi step thing that is a dynamic warmup.

01:19:29.458 --> 01:19:31.778
We've already checked balance off because we're hopping.

01:19:31.954 --> 01:19:35.218
We check stability up because we're hopping on one leg. Right.

01:19:35.354 --> 01:19:40.578
Reaction time because we're listening to the coach's cue and it's pretty fun. It's also pretty safe.

01:19:40.674 --> 01:19:41.922
Not a huge injury risk there.

01:19:41.946 --> 01:19:45.122
There's not a high load or velocity or fatigue.

01:19:45.266 --> 01:19:49.218
Not multiple steps when the kid isn't ready for it yet. Pretty simple instructions.

01:19:49.314 --> 01:19:54.742
I think most 7 year olds, regardless of athletic ability, we'd be able to be execute that just fine.

01:19:54.806 --> 01:19:56.086
And you do that for 10 minutes.

01:19:56.198 --> 01:20:00.130
Right now reality is you'd probably do two or three or four or five of those games.

01:20:00.510 --> 01:20:05.302
But I just picking you one for an example from the warmup, you then move on to step number two

01:20:05.326 --> 01:20:08.070
or stage number two, which is coordination.

01:20:08.230 --> 01:20:10.250
So the focus is to work on coordination.

01:20:10.590 --> 01:20:14.050
The more specific goal is movement efficiency and power.

01:20:14.350 --> 01:20:16.422
Maybe spend 15 minutes here.

01:20:16.606 --> 01:20:18.502
The coaching is fast and loose, right?

01:20:18.526 --> 01:20:19.670
You're picking things up a little bit.

01:20:19.710 --> 01:20:23.100
You're trying to be competitive because you want them to work on some power.

01:20:23.140 --> 01:20:26.060
So they got to move with high intent exercises.

01:20:26.220 --> 01:20:32.076
Things like a bear crawl through different hoops, hula hoop to a cone throw drill.

01:20:32.108 --> 01:20:35.676
So you're literally doing a hula hoop and then doing a throw relay.

01:20:35.788 --> 01:20:37.052
Maybe multiple kids at a time.

01:20:37.076 --> 01:20:38.604
