So just use some common sense here and it should be fine.

01:23:16.682 --> 01:23:18.538
Kids are meant to jump and land.

01:23:18.674 --> 01:23:20.506
They do it all the time, all day.

01:23:20.658 --> 01:23:22.890
Normal plyometrics are not any different from that.

01:23:22.930 --> 01:23:25.754
So don't want to spend too much more time on that.

01:23:25.922 --> 01:23:32.660
I do want to spend a little more time though, specifically on what I call the 10 rules of lifting weights for kids.

01:23:33.240 --> 01:23:35.104
You know the concepts already.

01:23:35.272 --> 01:23:42.096
What are things that are specific to weights and free weights and strength training and resistance exercise for a kid?

01:23:42.208 --> 01:23:44.672
Let me run you through my personal list of 10.

01:23:44.856 --> 01:23:47.328
Number one, and stop me when you've heard this before.

01:23:47.464 --> 01:23:51.260
We're going to focus on quality, positivity and fun.

01:23:51.720 --> 01:23:58.020
I will give you numbers, but this is not how I have ever coached kids or, or ever had any of my students.

01:23:58.060 --> 01:24:03.972
And I've actually mentored more than a few master's students who went on and did their projects

01:24:04.036 --> 01:24:06.132
in this area of kids strength training.

01:24:06.196 --> 01:24:07.300
So I'm pretty familiar with the research.

01:24:07.340 --> 01:24:10.420
And again, speaking to many people, this is always about quality.

01:24:10.580 --> 01:24:16.280
Repetition ranges are really rough guidelines for adults and they're hyper, hyper rough guidelines for kids.

01:24:17.020 --> 01:24:19.332
I've made that point now and you're probably sick of it.

01:24:19.356 --> 01:24:23.550
But number one again, is that number two, something I have not brought up yet.

01:24:23.670 --> 01:24:25.650
Make sure the environment is safe.

01:24:26.230 --> 01:24:27.614
In other words, the equipment.

01:24:27.662 --> 01:24:30.910
Don't have kettlebells laying around, don't have dumbbells on benches.

01:24:30.990 --> 01:24:32.926
Kids will find a way to get hurt.

01:24:33.118 --> 01:24:38.222
So strength training will hurt kids if that part isn't taken care of.

01:24:38.326 --> 01:24:43.374
And way more important to do it in kids and youth than it is for adults.

01:24:43.422 --> 01:24:46.450
So make sure the environment is safe for those little squirrels.

01:24:46.870 --> 01:24:52.204
Number three, generally we're looking at for strength training, two to three times per week,

01:24:52.372 --> 01:24:54.924
20 to 30 minutes per session.

01:24:55.052 --> 01:24:58.844
Now the model I laid out a minute ago was probably more like 40 minutes, but that was a warm

01:24:58.892 --> 01:25:01.116
up and a cool down and different activities and games.

01:25:01.188 --> 01:25:05.916
The actual lifting part, you're probably talking about 20 to 30 minutes at max.

01:25:06.028 --> 01:25:08.716
Start off more conservative two days a week.

01:25:08.868 --> 01:25:11.244
As they get more competent, build up to three.

01:25:11.412 --> 01:25:15.200
Number four, full range of motion with good technique.

01:25:15.570 --> 01:25:19.386
Now that we're introducing load, we're going to put more of an emphasis on technique.

01:25:19.418 --> 01:25:23.510
When a kid is moving his own body, I'm going to let her do whatever she wants for the most part

01:25:23.810 --> 01:25:25.750
when she's putting a load on.

01:25:26.050 --> 01:25:28.042
Now more rules come into play.

01:25:28.226 --> 01:25:31.818
Not going to restrict everything and get everything really tight, but you get the point, right?

01:25:31.954 --> 01:25:36.794
More sense, more load on the Bar more load on the, on the implement, rather number five.

01:25:36.962 --> 01:25:41.230
We're always still going to start with those dynamic movements and we're going to try to instill

01:25:41.550 --> 01:25:43.702
warm ups and movement quality like that.

01:25:43.806 --> 01:25:49.478
Number six in terms of the actual number of sets, probably one to three sets with somewhere

01:25:49.494 --> 01:25:55.142
between six to 15 repetitions at a light load. What's light? I don't know.

01:25:55.166 --> 01:25:56.770
Call it less than 60%.

01:25:57.390 --> 01:26:01.050
Maybe counterintuitive, but you can do a one rep max with kids.

01:26:01.550 --> 01:26:03.558
You're not gonna do it for every exercise.

01:26:03.734 --> 01:26:05.446
A one rep max is not gonna be the same.

01:26:05.518 --> 01:26:09.132
You're not gonna rip smelling salts and, you know, really take them up to the end.

01:26:09.156 --> 01:26:14.844
But you can get a rough sense of what their maximal strength is in program like that. But think about it.

01:26:14.932 --> 01:26:21.820
You're doing two sets, 6 to 8 to 10 to 12 reps at 40, 50, 60% of their max.

01:26:21.980 --> 01:26:24.120
We're not here to maximize muscle growth.

01:26:24.660 --> 01:26:31.740
We're here to just use weights as a way to develop tendons, bones, muscles, movement capacity,

01:26:31.820 --> 01:26:34.910
motor skills, focus, movement literacy.

01:26:35.250 --> 01:26:37.130
It's just another tool in the tool bag.

01:26:37.210 --> 01:26:40.910
So it's a different outcome goal that we're looking at entirely.

01:26:41.730 --> 01:26:47.386
We'll increase load 5 to 10% as they become more competent.

01:26:47.498 --> 01:26:49.578
If they're not, then we don't.

01:26:49.754 --> 01:26:52.026
We're not going to hold tight to specific numbers.

01:26:52.138 --> 01:26:54.938
You're watching every repetition and you're.

01:26:55.034 --> 01:27:00.640
When you think the kid has learned enough for the day, then we're done for the day. That's all we're after.

01:27:00.940 --> 01:27:04.580
So we're not going to push more than moderate fatigue.

01:27:04.740 --> 01:27:09.300
You can push to pretty high fatigue when you're playing games and you're out there moving around.

01:27:09.340 --> 01:27:15.140
But when you got loads and we're strength training moderate fatigue at best at this age range.

01:27:15.300 --> 01:27:21.220
At times you can go past these numbers so you can get to maybe, you know, 80% of your one rep

01:27:21.260 --> 01:27:24.116
max in certain kids, if they're ready for it.

01:27:24.188 --> 01:27:28.292
But you're only going to dip into that pretty infrequently.

01:27:28.436 --> 01:27:30.132
And you're going to do it for small doses.

01:27:30.196 --> 01:27:32.852
You want to, you know, kind of run a check and see where they're at.

01:27:33.036 --> 01:27:37.012
You want to actually do some more advanced strength training because they've got, you know,

01:27:37.036 --> 01:27:39.380
two or three or four years behind their belt. Okay.

01:27:39.460 --> 01:27:40.964
But it's going to come in small doses.

01:27:41.012 --> 01:27:43.732
Most of your time is going to be spent at those much lighter loads.

01:27:43.876 --> 01:27:50.548
You can go at a lower repetition range below this 6 if you're working on technique or if you're

01:27:50.564 --> 01:27:51.920
working on speed and power.

01:27:52.540 --> 01:27:57.524
At that point, it makes sense to do three or five repetitions or something close to that.

01:27:57.692 --> 01:28:03.277
But for the most part, most of our time with these kids is going to be spent again, 1 to 2,

01:28:03.423 --> 01:28:10.980
3 at most sets per exercise, 6 to 12 reps at a pretty light load.

01:28:11.140 --> 01:28:15.252
Past that, we want to be working on some basic movement patterns.

01:28:15.316 --> 01:28:20.516
So you want to do upper body, you want to do and lower body rather, you want to do multi joint exercises.

01:28:20.628 --> 01:28:23.540
We're rarely doing isolation work, right?

01:28:23.580 --> 01:28:30.356
We're looking at squatting and hinging and pressing and pulling, horizontal, rotational, vertical, big movement groups.

01:28:30.388 --> 01:28:33.268
A couple of exercises per day and then get out of there.

01:28:33.404 --> 01:28:38.388
If you identified a particular weakness and asymmetry has developed or you're concerned about

01:28:38.444 --> 01:28:41.572
one, then you can add a couple of corrective exercises.

01:28:41.716 --> 01:28:46.298
Those can be a little bit more single joint, a little bit more isolation, depending on what's going on there.

01:28:46.354 --> 01:28:47.962
But that's the appropriate dose.

01:28:48.106 --> 01:28:52.730
So you add all that stuff together, you do it in a large variety, right?

01:28:52.770 --> 01:28:57.030
So we want to alter the stimulus, you want to alter the position and everything that we can.

01:28:57.410 --> 01:29:00.666
You're in and out, you know, in 20 to 30 minutes of actual lifting.

01:29:00.698 --> 01:29:05.290
You're doing that a couple of days a week. You're progressing somewhat conservatively.

01:29:05.450 --> 01:29:11.862
You do all those things, you're very, very likely to have a high quality result, low likelihood

01:29:11.926 --> 01:29:18.070
of negative consequences and injury, and you probably aren't likely to burn the kid out anytime soon.

01:29:18.190 --> 01:29:22.598
So those would be my again, 10 rules of lifting weights for kids.

01:29:22.774 --> 01:29:27.302
If we go past physical activity, which was where we started these guidelines, and then we went

01:29:27.326 --> 01:29:30.518
into strength training, I want to go all the way up to now.

01:29:30.574 --> 01:29:33.686
What about exercise and sports specifically?

01:29:33.878 --> 01:29:37.654
Generally, five recommendations here for youth athletes.

01:29:37.702 --> 01:29:41.148
Again, we're now talking about people in competitive, organized sports.

01:29:41.324 --> 01:29:47.644
Very first thing we want to recognize is you don't want to come into your sport after large

01:29:47.732 --> 01:29:50.720
periods of inactivity and then just start competing.

01:29:51.060 --> 01:29:53.868
What's the most common one you will see?

01:29:54.004 --> 01:29:58.732
University injuries in kids goes way up in the fall.

01:29:58.916 --> 01:30:03.468
Why kids go off in the summer. They don't do anything.

01:30:03.644 --> 01:30:04.892
They show right back up.

01:30:04.916 --> 01:30:09.408
They immediately jump into football practice or soccer practice or whatever they're doing.

01:30:09.544 --> 01:30:17.584
So they go from periods of really low activity to really high activity and boom, injuries start to happen. So don't do that.

01:30:17.752 --> 01:30:25.968
Number two, you still want to focus on foundational movements more and skill development less direct.

01:30:26.024 --> 01:30:32.176
Example, let's say that you're getting ready for football season or basketball, baseball season.

01:30:32.248 --> 01:30:35.380
Whatever the case is, we're in baseball season right now, spring training.

01:30:35.800 --> 01:30:40.976
The mistake here would be oh, I got hurt, my shoulder got hurt. Oh really? What happened?

01:30:41.008 --> 01:30:44.700
Well, I threw a bunch, you know, getting ready for season.

01:30:45.240 --> 01:30:48.500
So throwing a baseball would be a sport specific preparation.

01:30:49.000 --> 01:30:56.448
But that said you should still spend more time working on running and jumping and sprinting and landing.

01:30:56.624 --> 01:31:03.934
So if your only preparation for season is the sport technique, you're not fundamentally developing

01:31:03.982 --> 01:31:08.670
everything you need to be to actually be ready for the loads that will happen in your sport.

01:31:08.830 --> 01:31:15.262
And so overemphasizing skill development and under emphasizing move and development is still

01:31:15.286 --> 01:31:18.530
going to potentially leave you in a spot for a bunch of problems.

01:31:18.950 --> 01:31:23.690
The rule of thumb here roughly is what was say two to one.

01:31:24.230 --> 01:31:30.088
You don't want to spend more than two times the amount of hours or volume or however you're

01:31:30.104 --> 01:31:33.528
thinking about it in sport as you do in playtime.

01:31:33.704 --> 01:31:38.180
If your sport to play breakdown is 3 to 1, 4 to 1, 5 to 1.

01:31:38.800 --> 01:31:43.260
Now we're going to have problems because we're under developing those foundational movements.

01:31:43.760 --> 01:31:48.220
If it's closer to at least 2 to 1, you still got an okay chance.

01:31:48.560 --> 01:31:54.494
Again, numbers will vary based on individual and age, but it's a really really so and and that

01:31:54.502 --> 01:31:58.174
number actually is directly from research that was actually been sussed out to figure out like

01:31:58.182 --> 01:32:03.230
that is seems to be the number kids that have a higher ratio of the of 2 to 1 sport to play

01:32:03.350 --> 01:32:04.810
end up getting hurt more often.

01:32:05.350 --> 01:32:09.810
So important to know there another important guideline for youth athletes.

01:32:10.230 --> 01:32:14.530
Similar things it's this is what we call no permanent seasons.

01:32:15.270 --> 01:32:19.294
Eight months is roughly the number you generally don't want to spend more than eight months

01:32:19.382 --> 01:32:23.706
in a year playing a single sport. You see this routinely.

01:32:23.738 --> 01:32:29.802
Kids go through their their local school season and they go into special season or AAU or club

01:32:29.906 --> 01:32:32.778
and they end up having these like perpetual seasons.

01:32:32.874 --> 01:32:37.050
We have seen this with golfers, we have seen this with baseball players and volleyball players.

01:32:37.170 --> 01:32:41.418
We've seen this with runners, cross country going into track and then off season and indoor

01:32:41.594 --> 01:32:45.630
and you end up at a 12 month long season for kids.

01:32:46.020 --> 01:32:47.520
Eight months is the rule.

01:32:47.940 --> 01:32:51.132
Can't exactly tell you why, but the research is going to say that's the breaking point where

01:32:51.156 --> 01:32:54.188
longer than that we start seeing a greater increase in injury risk.

01:32:54.244 --> 01:32:58.440
So something to keep your kid aware of at that point.

01:32:59.060 --> 01:33:02.480
Lastly, here's what we call no early specialization.

01:33:03.220 --> 01:33:08.588
Below 16 years old kids should be participating in more than one sport.

01:33:08.684 --> 01:33:13.254
After that, if you want to specialize to one, you can, but, but the more sports the better.

01:33:13.422 --> 01:33:17.846
And at minimum 2 below the age of 16 if you can pull it off.

01:33:17.998 --> 01:33:23.350
At this point I've probably gotten myself into a lot of hot water regarding this specialization.

01:33:23.430 --> 01:33:28.550
I have actually talked about this in small parts before in the past and man, the Internet got mad.

01:33:28.710 --> 01:33:32.982
And so I do want to make sure we're all really clear when we talk about early specialization

01:33:33.046 --> 01:33:34.850
in sports, since we're right here.

01:33:35.230 --> 01:33:41.220
Firstly, it does not harm your ability to become an elite athlete by playing multiple sports.

01:33:41.720 --> 01:33:47.740
I can say this a bunch of different ways, but that is obnoxiously clear.

01:33:48.280 --> 01:33:55.900
If you have your kid play more sports, it will not compromise their ability to be a world champion at all.

01:33:56.840 --> 01:33:58.512
Pick the data point you want here.

01:33:58.616 --> 01:34:03.072
There's a fun thing that happens every year during the super bowl where they will report right

01:34:03.096 --> 01:34:08.496
before the game the amount of people playing in the super bowl, how many were multi sport athletes

01:34:08.528 --> 01:34:10.860
and how many only played football.

01:34:11.240 --> 01:34:13.180
You want to guess what that number is?

01:34:14.120 --> 01:34:20.272
It varies year to year, but it's Generally somewhere between 91 to 96% of the athletes on those

01:34:20.296 --> 01:34:22.420
rosters were multi sport athletes.

01:34:22.760 --> 01:34:25.460
So that point alone should end the conversation.

01:34:26.040 --> 01:34:33.382
Very rarely do people become world champions at the highest level in sports by only doing one sport.

01:34:33.446 --> 01:34:36.570
In fact, the data will show the exact opposite.

01:34:37.390 --> 01:34:39.570
Second point, if I didn't convince you right there.

01:34:40.350 --> 01:34:48.690
Specific studies have shown kids who play three or more sports from ages 11 to 15 are more likely

01:34:49.150 --> 01:34:55.730
to play in national versus club level teams by the time they get to the ages of 16 to 18.

01:34:56.520 --> 01:35:01.296
So if you have your 12 year old and you think your 12 year old is something special, hopefully

01:35:01.328 --> 01:35:03.936
they are all the best of luck to you.

01:35:04.088 --> 01:35:09.760
But if you want that 12 year old to reach the highest level possible, have that 12 year old

01:35:09.800 --> 01:35:11.888
playing in three or more sports.

01:35:12.064 --> 01:35:15.920
That will give you a stronger chance to be truly elite by the time that 12 year old turns 16

01:35:15.960 --> 01:35:20.540
or 18 than staying in the one sport routinely shown. All right.

01:35:20.840 --> 01:35:28.578
Now obviously there's some correlation there and associations, but second piece of evidence, right?

01:35:28.634 --> 01:35:32.066
Third piece of evidence, a recent meta analysis.

01:35:32.098 --> 01:35:34.910
And this is really about the most concrete thing I can tell you.

01:35:35.210 --> 01:35:39.922
This Meta analysis covered 51 different studies over the course of it.

01:35:39.946 --> 01:35:41.794
It was about 6,000 kids.

01:35:41.922 --> 01:35:48.070
And what's cool about it is it included almost 800 world top performers.

01:35:48.420 --> 01:35:52.060
So who actually becomes the best or close in the world?

01:35:52.180 --> 01:35:57.196
World top performers usually differentiated as things like again, who made the world championships

01:35:57.228 --> 01:36:04.860
versus only national championships, who made Olympic teams versus just national teams, who made Club teams versus hiring.

01:36:04.940 --> 01:36:07.400
So like that's oftentimes how they break these things down.

01:36:08.100 --> 01:36:10.760
Handful of things that came out of that meta analysis 1.

01:36:12.020 --> 01:36:16.040
The best kids played the most multi sports.

01:36:16.380 --> 01:36:25.080
It's just really clear you will not stop your kid's ability to be the best possible by making them play multiple sports.

01:36:25.500 --> 01:36:30.980
In fact, more likely you're giving them a better chance to become elite by giving them a more

01:36:31.020 --> 01:36:34.708
well rounded experience physically and cognitively.

01:36:34.804 --> 01:36:40.268
In my opinion that is the most important stuff for us to understand for exercising kids.

01:36:40.404 --> 01:36:45.548
We talked about just from a general physical activity perspective, from a lifting weights, directly

01:36:45.644 --> 01:36:49.500
from a motor development, from an overall sport perspective.

01:36:49.660 --> 01:36:56.240
The last little tidbit I wanted to throw at you here was what we know about how to motivate kids.

01:36:57.060 --> 01:37:00.556
You could use a different term if you'd like, but how to just get kids to be more physically

01:37:00.588 --> 01:37:01.932
active when they don't want to be.

01:37:02.036 --> 01:37:05.868
Some kids are just more into movement and sports and others are not.

01:37:05.924 --> 01:37:09.134
What do you do if you're that parent thinking how do I get them moving?

01:37:09.182 --> 01:37:10.558
I don't want to push them too much. Right?

01:37:10.614 --> 01:37:14.718
You don't want to do that negative thing and have them get mad and so on and so forth.

01:37:14.734 --> 01:37:16.718
What's the right amount of pushing? What are some strategies?

01:37:16.814 --> 01:37:19.774
Well, number one, just be more active yourself.

01:37:19.822 --> 01:37:20.942
That is always a good thing.

01:37:20.966 --> 01:37:24.558
If your kid is not active and you are not active, that is the easiest way.

01:37:24.614 --> 01:37:27.326
Kids will model parents, so kids will model community.

01:37:27.478 --> 01:37:32.638
So that said, the second one is get them in more environments where more kids and more adults are moving.

01:37:32.814 --> 01:37:39.522
If your community, your friends, your family is on tv, is on phones and stuff all day, they're going to mimic that.

01:37:39.546 --> 01:37:42.354
If they're just in different environments, they will mimic that.

01:37:42.442 --> 01:37:44.514
Those are the two biggest steps you can take.

01:37:44.682 --> 01:37:48.178
You yourself as well as get them in peer groups of kids who are more active.

01:37:48.274 --> 01:37:51.138
Doesn't mean you can't let them be friends with their kids who want to play video games all day anymore.

01:37:51.154 --> 01:37:52.306
But you get the point.

01:37:52.458 --> 01:37:57.842
Put them around environments where people are just more active. 3 and this is actually something

01:37:57.866 --> 01:38:02.448
that has worked a lot with my kid. Try different physical environments.

01:38:02.634 --> 01:38:09.620
This means play on the ground, play in the snow, play in the water, go into the forest, go into

01:38:09.660 --> 01:38:12.068
playgrounds, going to gyms, going to courts.

01:38:12.164 --> 01:38:13.604
Get them in different environments.

01:38:13.652 --> 01:38:18.532
Maybe they hate sport courts, but they love to play around in the forest. Great.

01:38:18.716 --> 01:38:23.908
Maybe they are cold and they hate the outdoors. Awesome. Try an indoor pool.

01:38:24.084 --> 01:38:28.260
Whatever you can do to change the physical environment, maybe something will Resonate and hit with them there.

01:38:28.300 --> 01:38:37.126
That hasn't resonated in the sports that you liked or the activities that your spouse or your family liked. Another thing, similar idea. Try different movement modes.

01:38:37.318 --> 01:38:44.050
If they don't like running because they don't like playing soccer, they don't like playing football or basketball. Try swimming. Try in the water.

01:38:44.510 --> 01:38:45.846
Make them move their body differently.

01:38:45.878 --> 01:38:49.190
Put them on a bike, put them on a board. Have them do skateboarding.

01:38:49.350 --> 01:38:51.302
Do different things that are different.

01:38:51.486 --> 01:38:54.134
Not just you moving your own body.

01:38:54.302 --> 01:38:55.558
Other stuff may do it.

01:38:55.614 --> 01:39:02.214
I can tell you right now, my daughter absolutely goes bonkers over swimming. Great. I hate swimming. Hate it personally.

01:39:02.262 --> 01:39:04.806
Don't like it at all. She's all into it. Fantastic.

01:39:04.838 --> 01:39:08.278
I will take that kid to the pool anytime she wants to go and we can take her there. Right?

01:39:08.414 --> 01:39:16.598
Her brother doesn't like it at all. Awesome. Whatever it takes. Different environments, similar concept. Try different implements.

01:39:16.774 --> 01:39:23.264
So bats, different rackets, different balls, long things, short things, sticks, flags, whatever.

01:39:23.312 --> 01:39:26.100
You're getting the point, right? Just try different exposures.

01:39:26.760 --> 01:39:30.900
Try another thing that's worked a lot. Try different play partners.

01:39:31.400 --> 01:39:36.592
One thing that happens with kids being so far behind other kids, if they're around their peers

01:39:36.656 --> 01:39:40.820
only, they tend to withdraw and fall back if they're below them or delayed.

01:39:41.320 --> 01:39:46.260
So having them always compete against the same kids who are better than them may not be your best option.

01:39:46.600 --> 01:39:48.150
Play with them directly yourself.

01:39:48.280 --> 01:39:51.226
A lot of little kids don't want to play with other kids for that reason.

01:39:51.378 --> 01:39:52.954
But they will play with you.

01:39:53.122 --> 01:39:54.682
So you'll be the one to play catch with them.

01:39:54.706 --> 01:39:56.394
You'll be the one to play the sports with them.

01:39:56.482 --> 01:40:00.330
And not only are you modeling the behavior, but they actually are more comfortable not being

01:40:00.370 --> 01:40:02.698
good at something with you because they don't have any reference point.

01:40:02.754 --> 01:40:04.122
They know mom and dad are better.

01:40:04.226 --> 01:40:05.482
They just know uncle's going to be better.

01:40:05.506 --> 01:40:06.938
They know auntie's going to be better.

01:40:07.074 --> 01:40:09.162
So they don't have that negative experience.

01:40:09.266 --> 01:40:11.990
Being the worst one in the group like that.

01:40:12.530 --> 01:40:14.656
If they have siblings, same thing.

01:40:14.728 --> 01:40:19.120
Maybe don't have them play with the sibling if it's causing that negative dynamic.

01:40:19.280 --> 01:40:20.832
Or cousin or whatever the case is.

01:40:20.856 --> 01:40:24.260
Or the neighbor, so on and so forth. Or themselves.

01:40:24.600 --> 01:40:27.344
Maybe the best scenario is have them play with themselves.

01:40:27.392 --> 01:40:31.504
More practice by themselves rather than in front of the other ones if that's causing the problem.

01:40:31.672 --> 01:40:40.634
Finally, try to make things a differentiator between task goals versus sport goals. Here's what I mean.

01:40:40.722 --> 01:40:45.070
Maybe the kid has no interest in sports, but you can get them to build a Fort.

01:40:45.490 --> 01:40:52.426
You can get them to do something where, hey, we got to get all these rocks into the water over there. Great.

01:40:52.498 --> 01:40:55.882
They're still going to be playing sports, but they don't care about winning, they don't care

01:40:55.906 --> 01:40:59.034
about points on the board, but they will do something like that.

01:40:59.202 --> 01:41:02.730
Maybe it wasn't the best example, but hopefully you can come up with your own example there

01:41:02.770 --> 01:41:05.658
where you're getting them to do different things.

01:41:05.794 --> 01:41:07.350
Have a start, a stop.

01:41:07.810 --> 01:41:08.762
Here's what we're trying to do.

01:41:08.786 --> 01:41:11.146
We're going to accumulate points, but the points are different.

01:41:11.298 --> 01:41:14.554
It has an outcome that they like and it's not just reward based.

01:41:14.642 --> 01:41:17.098
It is just actual intrinsic reward.

01:41:17.274 --> 01:41:21.706
We did our thing, but the thing is now not a sport or a score. It is great.

01:41:21.778 --> 01:41:23.962
We build that cool deck. Awesome.

01:41:24.026 --> 01:41:26.218
We put that bridge across the water.

01:41:26.274 --> 01:41:29.322
Now we can go all the way over. You get the idea.

01:41:29.466 --> 01:41:32.836
I hope that was informative and I gave something for everybody.

01:41:32.978 --> 01:41:36.912
We try to do our best to stick to what we know from the literature as it stands.

01:41:36.976 --> 01:41:41.520
I also wanted to cover a lot of different options to meet you at your individual needs, your

01:41:41.560 --> 01:41:46.528
kid at their individual needs and make sure we've got tools and tactics for everybody.

