01:44:45.896 --> 01:44:49.088
And again, it's called the Future of Play Initiative.

01:44:49.264 --> 01:44:53.936
If I had to take these last couple of hours and summarize them all down to 10 things, here's what I'd say.

01:44:54.088 --> 01:45:00.940
Number one, unfortunately, we are just in a position in our human history where your kids need

01:45:01.640 --> 01:45:06.590
specific exercise and strength conditioning for their short and long term health.

01:45:06.710 --> 01:45:10.862
And this is probably the first generation where I would truly say that, but I just don't think

01:45:10.886 --> 01:45:12.142
we can argue that anymore.

01:45:12.286 --> 01:45:18.382
Number two, while many people, and probably my circle knows a lot of the things I talked about

01:45:18.406 --> 01:45:22.526
or at least conceptually agreed with me, we still need to keep spreading the word.

01:45:22.638 --> 01:45:27.502
This is a very important topic to share, as evidenced by the amount of misinformation that is

01:45:27.526 --> 01:45:33.200
out there, the misconceptions and some of the things that are still prevalent in the scientific,

01:45:33.360 --> 01:45:37.920
clinical and practical field regarding specifically strength training in kids.

01:45:38.080 --> 01:45:45.840
Number three, always, always make sure whatever you're doing, you're promoting a positive relationship

01:45:46.000 --> 01:45:50.020
with human movement, physical activity and exercise in kids.

01:45:50.600 --> 01:45:59.300
Number four, differentiate between developing an athlete and making sure we're developing overall athleticism.

01:46:00.170 --> 01:46:03.470
Everybody has a body, so everybody's an athlete in their own right.

01:46:03.770 --> 01:46:07.538
This may look, feel and smell different for everybody. That's fine.

01:46:07.714 --> 01:46:14.430
Nobody has to play sports, but everybody should have the ability to be athletic. Number five.

01:46:15.050 --> 01:46:17.458
This is all about movement literacy for life.

01:46:17.514 --> 01:46:19.990
We want big movement vocabularies.

01:46:20.410 --> 01:46:22.242
You can solve a lot of problems.

01:46:22.306 --> 01:46:24.082
You can give yourself a lot of options.

01:46:24.226 --> 01:46:27.180
The bigger your vocabulary is. Number six.

01:46:27.560 --> 01:46:30.740
You start maximizing that vocabulary at birth.

01:46:31.240 --> 01:46:36.660
Of course, everything is appropriate to age, desire and abilities.

01:46:37.160 --> 01:46:41.540
But this thing starts as soon as we can move because we've started moving.

01:46:41.880 --> 01:46:46.064
So we need to start working on movement and we need to get better at movement. Number seven.

01:46:46.232 --> 01:46:47.856
Each kid is their own athlete.

01:46:47.888 --> 01:46:52.140
They are one of one. It's not always possible. I get it.

01:46:52.180 --> 01:46:54.364
I've taught summer camps before.

01:46:54.532 --> 01:47:00.360
I've taught kids, I've taught the middle school. 50 plus groups of kids at a time.

01:47:00.660 --> 01:47:05.212
You gotta do group stuff, you gotta bucket however you can. I get it. That's realistic.

01:47:05.276 --> 01:47:11.560
But when possible, try to meet every kid where they are at and treat them as a one of one.

01:47:12.340 --> 01:47:15.292
Number eight, all this starts with you.

01:47:15.476 --> 01:47:20.150
The more you can do, the more you can model, the more you can help promote these activities,

01:47:20.340 --> 01:47:25.310
the more likely it is your kid or your nephew, your neighbor is going to have success in those things.

01:47:25.690 --> 01:47:29.986
Number nine, don't over specialize, especially early.

01:47:30.138 --> 01:47:35.910
We can do that later in life, probably a lot later than you think. There are some exceptions.

01:47:36.410 --> 01:47:39.378
Gymnastics peaks at like 12. Yes, I get it.

01:47:39.434 --> 01:47:40.530
I hear you out there.

01:47:40.650 --> 01:47:45.682
But for the most part, as a general rule, we want to specialize later in life and spend more

01:47:45.706 --> 01:47:50.732
of our time on foundational basic human movement principles and concepts and loss on sports

01:47:50.796 --> 01:47:53.420
tactics and skills for younger kids.

01:47:53.580 --> 01:48:01.900
And 10th and finally, if we can fundamentally frame this as games and not drills, as fun and

01:48:01.940 --> 01:48:04.320
not workouts and not exercises.

01:48:05.380 --> 01:48:06.844
We re switch our brain.

01:48:06.892 --> 01:48:12.124
We take a kid centric rather than a adult centric approach to how we think about how we program,

01:48:12.212 --> 01:48:15.930
how we implement and how we coach strength training in kids.

01:48:16.100 --> 01:48:19.358
We're going to be more effective in the short and long term.

01:48:19.494 --> 01:48:21.310
I want to leave you on one final thought.

01:48:21.430 --> 01:48:27.086
And that is a quote from former President of the United States, Franklin D. Roosevelt from 1940.

01:48:27.158 --> 01:48:34.814
And he said, effectively, we can't always build a future for our youth, but we can build our youth for the future.

